An optimal and clean approach to solve this problem is using dynamic programming.

### Method Explanation

We can define $dp[t][u]$ as the minimum cost to reach city $u$ in exactly $t$ minutes.

- **Base Case**: At time $t = 0$, we are at city $0$, so $dp[0][0] = \text{passingFees}[0]$, and all other states are initialized to $\infty$.
- **Transitions**: Since road travel times are at least $1$, time strictly increases with every edge traversed. For each minute $t$ from $1$ up to `maxTime`, we examine every bidirectional edge $(u, v, \text{time})$. If $t \ge \text{time}$, we can transition:
  - From $u$ to $v$: $dp[t][v] = \min(dp[t][v], dp[t - \text{time}][u] + \text{passingFees}[v])$
  - From $v$ to $u$: $dp[t][u] = \min(dp[t][u], dp[t - \text{time}][v] + \text{passingFees}[u])$
- **Result**: The answer is the minimum value among all $dp[t][n - 1]$ for $0 \le t \le \text{maxTime}$. If city $n - 1$ is never reached within `maxTime`, return `-1`.

With $\text{maxTime} \le 1000$ and $\text{edges.length} \le 1000$, the total number of operations is around $10^6$, which runs well within the time limit.

### Java Solution

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int minCost(int maxTime, int[][] edges, int[] passingFees) {
        int n = passingFees.length;
        int INF = 1_000_000_000;

        int[][] dp = new int[maxTime + 1][n];
        for (int i = 0; i <= maxTime; i++) {
            Arrays.fill(dp[i], INF);
        }

        dp[0][0] = passingFees[0];

        for (int t = 1; t <= maxTime; t++) {
            for (int[] edge : edges) {
                int u = edge[0];
                int v = edge[1];
                int time = edge[2];

                if (t >= time) {
                    if (dp[t - time][u] != INF) {
                        dp[t][v] = Math.min(dp[t][v], dp[t - time][u] + passingFees[v]);
                    }
                    if (dp[t - time][v] != INF) {
                        dp[t][u] = Math.min(dp[t][u], dp[t - time][v] + passingFees[u]);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        int minCost = INF;
        for (int t = 0; t <= maxTime; t++) {
            minCost = Math.min(minCost, dp[t][n - 1]);
        }

        return minCost == INF ? -1 : minCost;
    }
}
